      "text": "ment of ultrashort chain PFAS that are increasingly important in these waters. This contribution examines the rejection of PFAS and organic fab co- contaminants by NF membranes at both the lab and pilot scale in a lab solution and a fab wastewater matrix. Experiments with the lab solution were first performed using a flat sheet filtration cell to identify an appropriate NF membrane for rejection of both PFAS and co- A.M. Griffin et al. Water Research 262 (2024) 122111 3 contaminants, which was then used in a pilot scale system to treat the lab solution and fab wastewater. The evaluation of lab solutions and fab wastewater at lab and pilot scale with inclusion of relevant ultrashort chain compounds and co-contaminants fills critical data gaps and sup- ports the further deployment of membrane technologies for managing fab wastewater and other complex water matrices to minimize release of PFAS. 2. Materials and methods 2.1. Membranes evaluated Experiments were conducted using Filmtec NF270 and NF90 mem- branes that were either cut from a flat sheet provided by the manufac- turer or purchased as a spiral wound module. These commercial membranes have been previously used for PFAS treatment in other water matrices and are considered to be representative loose (NF270) and tight (NF90) NF membranes ( Liu et al., 2021 ; Safulko et al., 2023 )."
